    "lawCode": "Add Art 37 Title 4 §§37-0401 - 37-0407, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8932\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nregulation of toxic substances in playground surfacing materials\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo prohibit manufacturers from including PFAS, PAHs, or lead above 90\nparts per million as components of playground surfacing materials.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the environmental conservation law to\nprohibit manufacturers in the state from selling, distributing, or\noffering for sale playground surfacing materials including PFAS, PAHs,\nor quantities of lead above 90 parts per million. It requires manufac-\nturers to provide individuals who sell or distribute playground surfac-\ning materials in the state with a certificate of compliance. A civil\npenalty not to exceed $10,000 is imposed on individuals upon their first\nviolation of the provisions in this section. In the case of a second\nviolation, that penalty is not to exceed $25,000.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPlaygrounds are an essential part of childhood development. They offer a\nsafe place for children to explore, enhance social, emotional and phys-\nical learning, develop social skills, exercise, and build relationships.\nUnfortunately, many playgrounds are made with toxic materials, including\nPFAS, which undermine the health and safety of children and can stymie\ndevelopment.\n\nThis bill would prohibit manufacturers from including Per- and Polyfluo-\nrinated Substances (PFAS), lead in quantities above 90 parts per\nmillion, or Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs) as a component of\nplayground surfacing materials. All three of these substances are known\nto be harmful to humans, and the risk they pose to children is especial-\nly great. While there are many different kinds of PFAS, Perfluoroocta-\nnoic Acid (PFOA), the most common and well-studied kind, was classified\nby the. International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) Monograph\nProgram as carcinogenic to humans. Similarly, there is evidence that\nexposure to PAlis may increase the risk of cancer and cardiovascular\ndisease among other illnesses. Lead is especially infamous for its irre-\nversible harmful effects on children, causing increased antisocial\nbehavior, lowered success in school, anemia, and other negative health\nconsequences.\n\nIt is clear that children in New York should have as little exposure to\nlead, PFAS, and PAM as possible. And yet too often, playground surfacing\nmaterials have been found to contain all three. By prohibiting these\nsubstances from being included in playground surfacing materials, impos-\ning fines on individuals found in violation of this law, and requiring\nmanufacturers of playground surfacing materials to provide certificates\nof compliance, this bill would make playgrounds in the state safer,\nprotecting the health of the children of New York State.\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
